.TH DCANLOCK 8 "2026-10-28"
.SH NAME
dcanlock \- Diablo program to make and check Cancel-Keys and Cancel-Locks, and to write cancel articles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dcanlock
[
.B \-C
.I diablo.config
] [
.B \-A
.I dreader.access
] [
.B \-v
.I vserver
] [
.B \-u
.I user
] [
.B \-t
.I scheme
] [
.B \-S
]
.B key
|
.B lock
.I message-id
\&...
.br
.B dcanlock
[ options ] [
.B \-n
.I newsgroups
] [
.B \-f
.I from
] [
.B \-s
.I subject
] [
.B \-b
.I body
] [
.B \-c
.I charset
] [
.B \-d
] [
.B \-m
] [
.B \-a
] [
.B \-L
]
.B cancel
.I message-id
.br
.B dcanlock
.B verify
.I cancel-keys
.I cancel-locks

.SH DESCRIPTION
.IR dcanlock
works with the Cancel-Lock and Cancel-Key headers of RFC 8315, which
.BR dreaderd (8)
adds to the articles posted through it when the vserverdef in
.I dreader.access
has
.B canlockadmin
or
.B canlockuser
secrets, and checks before it executes a cancel or a Supersedes (the
.B readercanlock
option of
.IR diablo.config ).
The keys and locks are made by libcanlock, the library INN uses with its
.B canlockadmin
and
.B canlockuser
secrets, so
.B dcanlock key
does what INN's
.B "gencancel \-k"
does and
.B dcanlock cancel
what
.B gencancel
does.  It is built only when
.B CANLOCK_ENABLED
is defined in
.IR lib/vendor.h .
.PP
.B key
prints, for each Message-ID (with its angle brackets, one line for each),
the value of a Cancel-Key header: one
.IR scheme : key
element for each secret and scheme.  Put it into the Cancel-Key header of a
cancel control message (or of an article with a Supersedes header) and post
it: with the admin secrets this withdraws any article posted through
dreaderd, whoever posted it.  dreaderd never adds the admin key itself.
.PP
.B lock
prints the value of the Cancel-Lock header such an article carries, to check
what a post should have got.
.PP
.B cancel
writes a whole cancel article for the Message-ID to standard output: From,
Newsgroups, Subject, Date, a new Message-ID, Approved, the
.B Control: cancel
header, the Cancel-Key made from the admin secrets, and a short body.  It
is meant to withdraw any article that came from this site.  Post it through
dreaderd with a client that sends a file (the reader's access definition
needs
.B approve yes
if the Approved header is kept, and the user must be allowed to post control
messages), or feed it to the news server like any other article.  The lines
end in CR LF, as they go over NNTP (a client that dot-stuffs and sends the
terminating dot needs nothing else); the body is one line that does not start
with a dot.
.PP
.B verify
needs no secret: it exits with status 0 and prints
.I match
when one element of the first value is a key for an element of the second
value (the same scheme, base64 of the hash of the key), else it prints
.I "no match"
and exits with status 1.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B \-C diablo.config
The configuration file, as for the other programs.
.TP
.B \-A dreader.access
The file to read the secrets from; by default the one of
.IR diablo.config .
.TP
.B \-v vserver
The vserverdef the secrets are in; the default is
.IR DEFAULT .
.TP
.B \-u user
Use the
.B canlockuser
secrets and this user, as dreaderd makes them for an identified poster,
instead of the
.B canlockadmin
secrets.
.TP
.B \-t scheme
Make only
.IR sha256 ,
.I sha1
or
.I sha512
elements.  By default sha256 and sha1 are made, unless the vserverdef says
.B "canlocksha1 no" .
.TP
.B \-S
Read the secret from the first line of standard input and not from
.IR dreader.access ,
for a machine that has neither the file nor a
.IR diablo.config .
The secret is read from standard input, never from the command line, so it
doesn't show in the process list.  It is used as the
.B canlockadmin
secret, or as the
.B canlockuser
secret with
.BR \-u .
Both schemes are made unless
.B \-t
says otherwise; with
.B \-A
the
.B canlocksha1
line of the vserverdef in that file is honoured too.
.PP
Options of
.B cancel
only (the letters are those of gencancel):
.TP
.B \-n newsgroups
The Newsgroups of the cancel: a comma separated list.  By default those of
the article, read with
.BR dreadart (8)
from the spool (the one in the directory of this program); on a reader machine
without a spool, give them.  If the article's list does not fit the
program stops with an error and the groups must be given.
.TP
.B \-f from
The From; the default is
.B newsadmin
of
.IR diablo.config .
Plain ASCII only.
.TP
.B \-s subject
The Subject; the default is "cmsg cancel <message-id>".  Plain ASCII only:
MIME-encode anything else yourself.
.TP
.B \-b body
The text of the body; the default is "Admin cancel.".
.TP
.B \-c charset
The charset of the body; the default is ISO-8859-1.
.TP
.B \-d
Write the Date in UTC and not in local time.
.TP
.B \-m
Leave out the Message-ID; the server makes one.
.TP
.B \-a
Leave out the Approved header, which repeats the From.
.TP
.B \-L
Leave out the Cancel-Key header; no secret is needed then.

.SH EXAMPLE
Withdraw the article <1234@example.invalid>, which was posted through
dreaderd, from the news server's machine:
.PP
.nf
dcanlock \-f admin@example.invalid \-a cancel '<1234@example.invalid>' > cancel.txt
(then post cancel.txt)
.fi
.PP
Only the key, to put in by hand:
.PP
.nf
dcanlock key '<1234@example.invalid>'
.fi

.SH FILES
.I dreader.access
holds the secrets in clear: only root and news may read it, and so
.B dcanlock
must be run by one of them.  The access cache in the db directory has them
too.

.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on success (for verify: a match), 1 for verify when nothing matches, 2 for
a usage error or when a secret, the vserverdef, the Newsgroups or the file
is missing.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
dcancel(8),
dreadart(8),
dreaderd(8),
diablo-files(5)
